WaveGain v1.5.3
===============================================================================

Japanese documentation / 日本語版: [README.ja.md](README.ja.md)

Latest release: [v1.5.3](https://github.com/demupe/wavegain/releases/tag/v1.5.3)
(October 19, 2026). This README describes the current source tree.
5.1/7.1 surround support is unreleased and requires building this development
source; the v1.5.3 release supports mono/stereo only.

WaveGain measures and adjusts the loudness of mono, stereo and supported surround audio files.
It supports ReplayGain and BS.1770 integrated loudness (LUFS), with optional
true-peak protection. Gain is applied directly to the audio samples.

By default, WaveGain only calculates and reports gain. Use -y to apply gain
to the input, or add --output / --output-dir to retain the original files.
Applying gain and undoing it are lossy.

Features
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
* Track or album loudness adjustment using ReplayGain or integrated LUFS.
* Unreleased: 5.1/7.1 surround normalization with explicit layouts in LUFS mode.
* True-peak measurement and an explicit output ceiling.
* Separate output files, including recursive directory processing.
* WAV, RF64 and Sony Wave64; limited 16-bit AIFF/AIFC support.
* 8- to 32-bit integer PCM and floating-point audio, at 14 sample rates
  from 8 to 192 kHz.
* Legacy dither and an optional sample-rate-dependent dither family.

Supported formats and large files
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
The v1.5.3 release supports mono/stereo integer PCM and floating-point
audio in WAV and Wave64 at
8000, 11025, 12000, 16000, 22050, 24000, 32000, 44100, 48000, 64000, 88200,
96000, 176400 and 192000 Hz. Compressed codecs are unsupported. 16-bit integer
AIFF/AIFC input is also supported. -b selects the output sample format;
-b 6 selects 16-bit AIFF, which retains its 32-bit container-size limit.
The development source additionally supports the 5.1/7.1 layouts described
under Surround audio (unreleased), with --lufs or --target-lufs.

WAV / RF64:
* POSIX builds use 64-bit file offsets and process files larger than 4 GiB.
* WAV output larger than 4 GiB uses 0xFFFFFFFF RIFF/data sizes and emits a
  warning. --rf64 reserves a JUNK chunk and converts it to ds64 only when
  needed, so smaller output remains ordinary RIFF/WAV.
* --rf64 requires seekable output and is ignored with --stdout. RF64 input
  is supported and follows the same output-size rules when rewritten.
* On seekable input, sentinel sizes or data extending past EOF are read to
  the end of the file, provided it ends on a complete PCM frame. Incomplete
  frames are refused, including after EOF recovery. Stdin remains strict.

Sony Wave64:
* Input is detected by its GUIDs. Wave64 remains Wave64 when applying gain
  or changing PCM format, unless AIFF output is explicitly selected.
* Container/chunk sizes and sample counts use 64-bit fields. A format chunk
  must precede a single audio data chunk.
* Unknown chunks before and after audio are preserved with bounded-memory
  copying. 'fact' and 'gain' are rewritten before audio; stale 'levl' peak
  summaries are dropped.
* Seekable input with unfinalized or truncated sizes is accepted with a
  warning if the recovered audio ends on a complete PCM frame. Incomplete
  frames are refused. Stdin remains strict.
* There is no WAV-to-Wave64 output option; use an external converter to
  create the initial .w64 file.

The optional 'gain' chunk stores the applied scale for --undo-gain. It is a
WaveGain extension, not a player loudness tag. Wave64 uses the private GUID
(e217d848-96be-4ac0-b56a-8b46289c71e0) with a little-endian IEEE double.
On macOS, the WAV 'gain' scale is stored big-endian (an upstream choice), so
WAV undo metadata is not interchangeable with other platforms.

Use '-' for stdin in calculation mode. LUFS/true-peak stdin measurement also
requires -p and finalized container sizes. Applying gain requires file input.

Quick start
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Run these commands after building WaveGain, or use wavegain instead of
./wavegain if it is installed on your PATH.

Calculate ReplayGain without changing the input:

    ./wavegain input.wav

Apply ReplayGain to a separate file:

    ./wavegain -y --output normalized.wav input.wav

Normalize to -23 LUFS with a -1 dBTP ceiling:

    ./wavegain -y --target-lufs -23 --peak-ceiling -1 --output out.wav in.wav

Apply one album gain while preserving the tracks' relative loudness:

    ./wavegain -y -a --output-dir normalized track1.wav track2.wav

Process a directory recursively, with each folder treated as an album:

    ./wavegain -y -a -z --output-dir ../normalized ./album

Select 24-bit output with sample-rate-dependent medium noise shaping:

    ./wavegain -y -b 3 -d 3 --dither-profile rate --output out.wav in.wav

Store the applied gain for a later, lossy undo (these commands rewrite input):

    ./wavegain -y -w recording.w64
    ./wavegain --undo-gain recording.w64

Separate output never replaces an existing destination. --output accepts one
explicit input file; --output-dir supports multiple files and preserves
relative subdirectories during recursive processing. Its output root must be
outside the input directories. See the full option reference below.

On POSIX, separate output inherits the input's ordinary access permissions,
without copying owner/group or special mode bits. Where neither exclusive
rename nor hard links work (for example, some exFAT/SMB implementations),
output is copied into an exclusively created destination. That fallback can
be visible while copying, requires space for both files, and retains an
incomplete destination plus the completed temporary on failure.

Loudness, peaks and dither
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
ReplayGain is the default. It estimates perceived loudness using weighting
filters and audio energy rather than normalizing only the highest sample.
Track mode adjusts each file independently. Album mode applies one gain to
preserve relative loudness between tracks.

ReplayGain and LUFS: what differs?

Both help match perceived loudness between recordings. ReplayGain is a
loudness-normalization system; LUFS (Loudness Units relative to Full Scale)
is a measurement unit. In WaveGain, the choice is between the legacy
ReplayGain algorithm and BS.1770 integrated loudness measured in LUFS.

| In WaveGain | ReplayGain (default) | LUFS mode (--lufs) |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Frequency weighting | Legacy equal-loudness approximation (Yule/Butterworth filters) | BS.1770 K-weighting |
| Time measurement | 50 ms energy blocks; uses the 95th percentile, emphasizing louder passages | Integrated energy of overlapping 400 ms blocks; gates out silence and sufficiently quiet passages |
| Target | Legacy ReplayGain calibration; -g adds a manual dB adjustment | -23 LUFS by default; --target-lufs sets another target |
| Channels | Mono/stereo | Mono/stereo; supported 5.1/7.1 layouts in development source (unreleased) |

A LUFS reading describes loudness; the resulting gain is a change in dB.
For example, -18 LUFS measured against a -23 LUFS target calls for -5 dB
of gain before manual adjustment and peak protection. ReplayGain directly
reports the gain relative to its own reference. Different weighting,
statistics and targets mean that the two modes can produce different gains
for the same recording; there is no fixed offset that converts every legacy
ReplayGain result into integrated LUFS.

Use the default to retain WaveGain's established ReplayGain behavior. Use
LUFS for an explicit loudness target, comparison with BS.1770 meters, or
surround input. Both support track and album gain. Normalization applies a
constant gain per track or album; it does not itself compress the changing
volume within a recording. True peak is a separate peak measurement, and
--lufs alone does not enable --true-peak. When applying gain (-y), both modes change
PCM directly; WaveGain does not write player ReplayGain tags.

Some tools calculate ReplayGain tags using BS.1770 (often called ReplayGain
2.0). The ReplayGain column above describes WaveGain's legacy mode, not all
software that writes ReplayGain tags.

Sources: [ReplayGain calculation](https://replaygain.hydrogenaudio.org/calculating_rg.html),
[ITU-R BS.1770-5](https://www.itu.int/dms_pubrec/itu-r/rec/bs/R-REC-BS.1770-5-202311-I%21%21PDF-E.pdf),
and [EBU R 128](https://tech.ebu.ch/docs/r/r128.pdf).

--lufs selects BS.1770 integrated loudness with a default target of -23 LUFS.
--target-lufs changes that target and also selects LUFS mode. Album LUFS uses
a common gate over the tracks' block energies, rather than averaging their
LUFS values. LUFS needs at least 400 ms of audio; silence and material below
the absolute gate receive only the requested manual gain.

--true-peak enables true-peak measurement and a default ceiling of -1 dBTP.
--peak-ceiling sets a different ceiling and also enables true-peak measurement.
An explicit ceiling remains active with -n (--noclip). Quantized output is
measured again before file publication; exceeding the ceiling prevents
replacement. Peak prevention may leave output below the requested LUFS target.
With --stdout, already-emitted audio cannot be retracted; a failed final check
returns a nonzero exit status.

--true-peak-mode precise (default) uses 16x, 385-tap interpolation for lower
high-frequency under-read. --true-peak-mode fast uses 4x, 49-tap interpolation
at EVERY supported rate, including 192 kHz. Either option enables true peak.
Both analyze every frame and use the selected meter for input and quantized
output; ReplayGain/LUFS arithmetic is unchanged. This differs from --fast,
which samples only part of the input and cannot be combined with true peak.
For example: wavegain -y --true-peak-mode fast input.wav

Fast mode trades peak accuracy for speed. Its under-read can exceed the 0.1 dB
reserve, so choose precise when high-frequency peak accuracy is important.
Neither finite FIR estimate guarantees every continuous-time reconstruction
or later sample-rate conversion; official conformance testing is pending.

DC correction is track-based by default; -q selects album DC correction and
-p disables it. With -p, LUFS and true-peak measurement share the first
analysis read. Measuring after DC correction requires another read.

Legacy ReplayGain analyzes 176.4/192 kHz input through a 2:1 low-pass FIR
decimator. LUFS and true peak use the source rate. Output sample rate and
frame count are preserved.

The default dither profile is legacy. --dither-profile rate requires -d 1..4
and integer output; it uses TPDF noise with optional first-order error
feedback adjusted to the sample rate. Existing ReplayGain rates and legacy
dither retain their original processing paths unless new options are selected.

The measurement engine bundles MIT-licensed libebur128 with C optimizations
inspired by R128Net, plus stereo block-energy SIMD and a peak-only processing
path. No installed libebur128 or .NET runtime is needed. Implementation,
licenses and precision contracts are documented in
doc/design/loudness-implementation.md.

Surround audio (unreleased)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
5.1 and 7.1 WAV, RF64 and Wave64 require --lufs or --target-lufs. Without
one of these options the file is warned and refused (exit status 1), and no
output is written for it. --undo-gain is exempt because it needs no loudness
analysis. Mono/stereo retain their default ReplayGain behavior.

    ./wavegain -y --lufs --true-peak --output surround-out.wav surround.wav

Supported WAVEFORMATEXTENSIBLE channel masks are 0x3f (5.1 back), 0x60f
(5.1 side) and 0x63f (7.1 side+back). Mask bits define the on-disk order;
outputs preserve both layout and order, including PCM width/float conversion.
Unknown, absent or inconsistent surround layouts are rejected; multichannel
AIFF/raw input, AIFF output and --limiter are outside this support contract.

BS.1770 loudness excludes LFE. Front L/R/C have energy weight 1.00; 5.1
surrounds and 7.1 sides use 1.41, while 7.1 backs use 1.00. The same chosen
gain is applied to every channel including LFE. Sample-peak prevention and
optional true-peak protection cover every channel, so LFE peaks can prevent
reaching the target LUFS. LFE-only/below-gate files use manual gain only.
No +10 dB LFE boost, crossover, downmix or bass management is performed.
Album DC means are grouped by speaker position across mixed layouts; the
historical mono/left convention remains for mono/stereo. Each surround file
reports all channel DC corrections by speaker label.

References: ITU-R BS.1770-5 Annexes 1/3 (including Table 5), ITU-R
BS.2217-2 channel-gain tests, and Microsoft's WAVEFORMATEXTENSIBLE contract.

Build and install
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Build from the repository root with a C compiler and make:

    make

The default release build uses the compiler's native architecture, including
64-bit targets. PCM storage uses fixed-width 32-bit types. For an explicit
32-bit x86 build, use make CFLAGS=-m32; this may require multilib headers and
libraries, such as gcc-multilib on Debian.

Install to /usr/local/bin, or override the prefix:

    sudo make install
    sudo make install prefix=/usr

The install target uses GNU-style install options. On macOS, the built binary
can also be copied to a directory on your PATH.

Windows builds can use MinGW-w64 GCC in an MSYS2 MINGW64 shell with the same
Makefile, or open mswindows/WaveGain.sln in Visual Studio 2022 or later (C17
support required). The modern project includes all src/*.c and supports Win32
and x64 with precise floating-point settings. MinGW release builds link libssp
statically for _FORTIFY_SOURCE and need only system DLLs. Output, measurement
and regression checks pass on native Windows ARM64 with LLVM-MinGW Clang;
x64 and native MSVC Win32/x64 checks are available through the shared inventory.
Run python tools/check_msvc.py from the matching VS Developer Command Prompt.
Other compiler/CPU combinations remain validation follow-ups. Debian packaging in debian/ is historical and
unmaintained; build from source rather than relying on the old debuild recipe.

Tests and development
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Repository layout:
    src/             Production C sources, headers and generated CLI help
    test/            Regression tests and audio fixtures
    tools/           Build, documentation and benchmark utilities
    vendor/          Bundled libraries and their licenses
    doc/             Design notes and generated manual
    mswindows/       Windows project files
    debian/          Debian packaging
    local-research/  Ignored local reports, measurements and experiments

Run builds from the repository root; the executable remains ./wavegain
(wavegain.exe on Windows).

    make debug              # build with additional compiler warnings
    make check              # run the regression gate (Python 3 required)
    make check-sanitize     # rebuild and test with ASan/UBSan
    make check-interop      # compare with FFmpeg (ffmpeg/ffprobe required)
    make verified-release   # release build with ReplayGain/loudness verification
    make check-loudness     # compare loudness kernels with native reference

Tests cover PCM/float conversion, gain/undo, metadata, malformed files, sparse
large files (including 5 GiB Wave64), output paths, loudness, true-peak ceilings,
resampling and dither signal properties. Open validation work is listed in TODO.
The shared test inventory and individual commands are documented in test/README.txt.
Use python test/run_suite.py --list to list groups, or --only NAME to select one.

verified-release compares ReplayGain filter state and complete conversion
output with a scalar build using the same compiler and flags, and removes
the binary on mismatch. It also verifies high-rate FIR streaming/output against
the scalar convolution and loudness kernels against the native reference.
VERIFY_SIMD=auto accepts either backend (default);
VERIFY_SIMD=1 requires paired stereo SIMD and VERIFY_SIMD=0 requires scalar.
GNU-compatible x86_64 builds also use handwritten SSE2 analysis kernels and
select the high-rate FIR's AVX2 kernel at runtime. Define
WAVEGAIN_X86_ASM=0 to retain C analysis kernels, or
WAVEGAIN_X86_AVX2=0 to verify the SSE2-only FIR. The FIR retains its
original scalar path on globally FMA-enabled builds to preserve rounding.

Release optimization can be selected without editing the Makefile. For Clang:
    make verified-release CC=clang RELEASE_OPT=-O3 LTO_FLAGS=-flto=thin
GCC can use LTO_FLAGS=-flto. Ordinary release builds retain -O2 with no LTO.
Do not enable fast-math: finite checks and exact filter arithmetic are required.

On native Windows, run the same regression gate without a POSIX make install:
    python tools/check_windows.py --cc aarch64-w64-mingw32-clang --optimized
Omit --optimized for -O2, or select x86_64-w64-mingw32-clang for x64.
Add --ubsan to check undefined behavior and float-to-integer overflow.
The x64 tests on an ARM64 host execute through Windows emulation.

For release-to-release byte comparisons and repeatable performance measurements:
    python test/pcm_equivalence.py --reference ./wavegain-before --candidate ./wavegain
    python tools/benchmark_native.py --reference ./wavegain-before --candidate ./wavegain --output timings.json
On Windows, use explicit .exe paths. These checks retain input files; benchmark
times use warm OS caches and do not establish cold-storage throughput.

Documentation index: doc/README.txt. Keep tracked documentation concise. Detailed
investigations, benchmark data and review histories belong in the gitignored
local-research/ directory, which is not included in clones or releases.

CLI help and manuals come from doc/help.json. After editing it, run make docs.
make check-docs verifies the generated README help, src/help_generated.h, man page
and HTML manual. Do not edit the option reference below directly.

Behavior changes
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Unreleased:
* Add explicit-layout 5.1/7.1 LUFS processing, all-channel peak protection,
  dither, format conversion and undo. Surround without LUFS is refused with
  a warning; legacy ReplayGain remains limited to mono/stereo.

Version 1.5.3:
* Input duplicate checks use invocation-wide path and file-ID hash indexes,
  avoiding quadratic lookup work across large recursive runs while retaining
  protection against aliases of original and rewritten files.
* Bundled LUFS/true-peak history and staging buffers cover rounded 400 ms
  blocks at arbitrary rates. The CLI's supported sample rates are unchanged.
* High-rate FFmpeg comparisons use faded signals; separate independent
  reconstruction checks retain coverage of abrupt end-of-file peaks.
* Windows --exec runs through the system cmd.exe with /d /v:on. References to
  WaveGain's variables are expanded after parsing, so special characters in
  file and folder names stay data. These variables do not expand inside pipes;
  commands containing '!' are refused, and carets in an argument that uses a
  variable act as escapes. Executable paths with spaces get one quote pair.

Version 1.5.2:
* DC analysis uses exact integer-PCM block sums and compensated block addition.
  Every nonzero requested gain/DC correction is applied, including adjustments
  below the former 0.1 dB and 16-bit DC thresholds. More files may be rewritten.
* Album DC uses time weighting per actual channel, so equal-duration material
  has equal weight regardless of storage sample rate.
* LUFS K-weighting uses cascaded biquads for improved high-rate stability.
  11025 Hz measurement uses exact 400 ms windows and fractional 100 ms hops.
* Unshaped legacy dither uses full-amplitude high-pass TPDF at every bit depth,
  including 24/32 bits. Dither remains off by default; shaped amplitudes remain.
* --true-peak-mode extended adds screened longer 16x reconstruction for
  transients, at higher CPU cost. It remains a finite estimate. Precise and
  fast retain their existing FIR interpolation.
* Reset all per-file legacy dither history and keep stereo noise-shaping phase
  aligned at partial blocks, preventing cross-format transients and tail errors.
* Preserve the signed 32-bit PCM minimum (-2147483648) instead of clamping it
  one LSB above the format's valid negative endpoint.
* By default, true-peak measurement uses 16x, 385-tap interpolation at every rate,
  reducing high-frequency under-read. Peak-limited gain/output can change;
  independent tone/reconstruction checks complement native kernel equality.
* High-rate ReplayGain FIRs explicitly disable FMA contraction. FMA-capable
  targets now retain the SSE2/AVX2 fast paths. Previously contracted FIR builds
  can change rounding; non-FMA builds preserve their existing arithmetic.
* Native MSVC builds use portable unused annotations and explicit filter
  precision controls. A modern Win32/x64 solution replaces the Intel project
  entry point; the older .vcproj/.icproj files are historical.

Version 1.5.1:
* GNU-compatible x86_64 builds keep ReplayGain filter histories in SSE2
  registers and evaluate eight high-rate FIR outputs in parallel, selecting
  AVX2 when available. Per-sample operation order, streaming state and output
  bytes are preserved; other targets retain their existing analysis paths.
* Reject non-invertible Wave64 undo scales, incomplete PCM frames and invalid
  extensible-WAV precision/extension lengths before rewriting input files.
  Incomplete frames are also refused in truncated files after EOF recovery.
* Reject repeated inputs (including file-ID aliases) before each processing
  batch to prevent repeated gain application. Earlier completed batches remain.
* Windows command construction is bounded by the shell limit, and command
  environment filenames no longer use a fixed-size copy. Album and command
  DC displays avoid narrowing large float-audio offsets to integers.
* ARM64 stereo ReplayGain filters retain paired histories and process both
  stages together. Peak/DC scans retain local accumulators. Filter arithmetic,
  DC summation order and generated audio bytes are preserved.
* Mono ReplayGain analysis reuses identical channel-filter results until stereo
  data is seen, preserving filter state, rounding and generated audio bytes.
* Windows recursive scans skip discovered directory links, including junctions.
  Explicit linked input roots remain usable. Output-directory checks resolve
  physical locations and reject aliases inside input or unverifiable locations.
* Faster integer PCM input conversion and high-rate analysis FIR history
  traversal preserve the decoded samples and filter accumulation order.
* Supported Clang/GCC x86_64 builds use fused stereo ReplayGain histories and
  four-frame true-peak interpolation. Dense 4x interpolation selects AVX2 at
  runtime when supported; other CPUs and interpolation layouts retain baseline
  SIMD. Results retain the reference operation order without requiring global
  AVX2 build flags.
* Integer gain application without dither/limiting uses a specialized path,
  preserving the legacy quantizer, DC correction and saturation boundaries.
* Tiny binary64 values in gain metadata serialize correctly on Windows x64.
* Windows directory diagnostics retain the original error code and safely
  handle literal percent signs and unavailable system messages.

Version 1.5.0:
* Separate output paths preserve originals and refuse existing destinations.
  Ordinary input permissions are retained on POSIX; filesystems without
  exclusive rename or hard links use an exclusive-copy fallback.
* LUFS can be selected instead of ReplayGain, with pooled album gating.
* True-peak ceilings remain active with --noclip and are checked after
  quantization; they can prevent reaching a requested LUFS target.
* 176.4/192 kHz input and an optional rate-aware dither profile are supported.

Earlier releases and compatibility notes:
* WAV headers are updated at validated chunk boundaries. Opaque metadata
  containing strings such as "fmt " is preserved, and odd-length RIFF chunks
  and data padding are handled when reading and converting sample formats.
* 16-bit AIFF/AIFC can be converted to WAV. Unsupported 8-bit AIFF/AIFC and
  malformed headers or gain chunks are rejected before replacing the input.
* Analysis or conversion failures now produce a nonzero exit status, including
  empty audio and partial batch failures. Deliberately skipped processed files
  still succeed. Output finalization checks metadata copies and header writes;
  failed conversions preserve the input. Windows replacement no longer deletes
  the input first, and temporary files are created exclusively.
* Extreme float/undo amplification is saturated before integer quantization
  instead of overflowing the quantizer or noise-shaping history.
* Clipping prevention and the reported Peak use the peak AFTER the selected
  DC offset correction (track or album), instead of the uncorrected peak.
* DC offset correction is applied when the offset of either channel exceeds
  1/32768 of full scale. Earlier releases required at least 2/32768.
* With -t (--limiter) every file is rewritten, even when no gain change or
  DC correction would otherwise be needed.
* 88.2 kHz files use new filter coefficients (the former ones were unstable),
  so their gain values change. Other rates can also change on FMA-capable
  builds (next item). MSVC builds using the legacy SSE2 filter (HAVE_SSE2)
  previously ignored the last Yule coefficient, so they change at every rate.
* The ReplayGain filters are evaluated without fused multiply-add (FMA). On
  FMA-capable builds (AArch64, or x86_64 with -march=x86-64-v3 or later) the
  filter rounding differs from earlier builds that let the compiler fuse
  operations, so at any sample rate a reported gain close to a 0.01 dB
  histogram step can move by one step (e.g. -0.44 dB -> -0.45 dB at 48 kHz).
  Builds that cannot use FMA (e.g. the default x86_64 -O2) give the same
  results as before. Within one build the scalar and SIMD filters match
  bit-for-bit; gain values matched across the compilers and targets tested
  for this change, but RMS accumulation and log10 are outside this rule, so
  identical gains across all compilers and architectures are not guaranteed.

Project history and references
